# Target C-II — the degree-pure support-six reciprocal `H6` branch is impossible

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the noncollapsed degree-pure support-six one-anchor endpoint after the convex `H7` exclusion  
**Depends on:** `target_c_support6_reciprocal_h7_exclusion_2026-09-18.md`; closed-equilateral-pentagon two-turn inequality; local `2+3` reciprocal sign alternation; bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_reciprocal_h6_exclusion_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The support-six `H7` theorem leaves, on the noncollapsed branch, reciprocal hull sizes
five and six. This note eliminates the six-vertex hull branch exactly.

The new ingredient is a ground-equilibrium order lemma. Six distinct unit rays in one
open semicircle, with three positive and three negative signs and zero signed sum, have
only one possible sign word up to global reversal:

\[
\boxed{+--++-}.
\]

Combining that word with the already proved local `2+3` alternation rule at the five
non-ground hull vertices leaves no rank-three order type for a convex hexagon plus one
interior reciprocal point. The finite check is an exact oriented-matroid enumeration:
there are 26 uniform single-element extensions of the convex-hexagon chirotope, and none
admits a compatible perfect matching and edge signing.

The only nonuniform configuration not covered by that first enumeration is the
collinearity already allowed by the reciprocal construction: the interior point, the
ground point, and the interior point's missing-matching mate can lie on one line. That
case is also finite. There are 14 simple collinearity walls between the 26 uniform order
types, and none admits a compatible signing once the necessary ground sign condition is
imposed.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{a noncollapsed degree-pure support-six block cannot have reciprocal hull size }6.
}
\]

Together with the previous convex-`H7` exclusion, every noncollapsed degree-pure
support-six block is now forced into reciprocal hull size five. The separate collapsed
branch `y_1=\cdots=y_6\ne0` remains live, so the global `n_1/6` corank coefficient is not
yet improved.

---

## 1. `H6` setup

Use the support-six notation from the preceding theorem. The six one-anchor pentagons
have relation graph

\[
R=K_6\setminus M,
\]

where `M` is a perfect matching on the six non-ground reciprocal vertices. Adding the
ground reciprocal point gives

\[
H=K_7\setminus M.
\]

Assume the noncollapsed branch, so the six non-ground reciprocal points are pairwise
distinct.

In the `H6` case label the reciprocal hull cyclically

\[
0,1,2,3,4,5,
\]

with `0` the ground point, and label the unique interior non-ground point `6`.
The previous theorem gives:

1. the ground hull angle satisfies `phi_0<2pi/3`;
2. the six ground edges are all present and the ground equilibrium has sign split
   exactly `3+3`;
3. every non-ground hull vertex `1,...,5` has a `2+3` sign split on its five incident
   reciprocal edges; and
4. at each such vertex, for either fixed global sign color, the local degree-two class
   occupies raw-ray positions two and four after the missing matching edge is deleted.

The last statement is the local alternation theorem inherited from the source contact
pentagon.

---

## 2. Ground order lemma for a `3+3` equilibrium in a semicircle

Let

\[
u_j=e^{i\theta_j},\qquad
\theta_1<\theta_2<\cdots<\theta_6,
\qquad
\theta_6-\theta_1<\pi,
\]

be six distinct unit vectors. Let `s_j in {+1,-1}` have three signs of each color and
assume

\[
\sum_{j=1}^6 s_j u_j=0.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Globally reverse signs if necessary so `s_1=+1`. Put

\[
\Delta_k=u_{k+1}-u_k,
\qquad
S_k=\sum_{j=1}^k s_j,
\qquad 1\le k\le5.
\]

Since the total signed count is zero, summation by parts gives

\[
\sum_{j=1}^6 s_j u_j
=-\sum_{k=1}^5 S_k\Delta_k.
\tag{2.2}
\]

The chord formula

\[
\Delta_k
=2\sin\frac{\theta_{k+1}-\theta_k}{2}
 \exp i\left(\frac{\theta_k+\theta_{k+1}}2+\frac\pi2\right)
\]

shows

\[
\arg\Delta_1<\arg\Delta_2<\cdots<\arg\Delta_5,
\]

and all five chord arguments lie in an open interval of width less than `pi`.
Consequently every nonzero positive combination of any consecutive collection of these
chords has argument strictly between the extreme chord arguments.

There are ten `3+3` words with first sign positive. Five have all prefix sums
`S_1,...,S_5` nonnegative and at least one positive:

```
+++---   ++-+--   ++--+-   +-++--   +-+-+-
```

For each, the right side of (2.2) is a nonzero positive combination of vectors in one
open halfplane, so it cannot vanish.

Four more words reduce to an impossible comparison of ordered chord arguments:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{word}&(S_1,\ldots,S_5)&\text{required identity}\\
\hline
++---+&(1,2,1,0,-1)&\Delta_5=\Delta_1+2\Delta_2+\Delta_3\\
+-+--+&(1,0,1,0,-1)&\Delta_5=\Delta_1+\Delta_3\\
+--+-+&(1,0,-1,0,-1)&\Delta_1=\Delta_3+\Delta_5\\
+---++&(1,0,-1,-2,-1)&\Delta_1=\Delta_3+2\Delta_4+\Delta_5.
\end{array}
\]

In the first two rows the right-hand side has argument strictly below
`arg Delta_5`; in the last two it has argument strictly above `arg Delta_1`.

The only word not contradicted is

\[
+--++-,
\]

whose prefix vector is `(1,0,-1,0,1)` and whose necessary chord relation is

\[
\Delta_3=\Delta_1+\Delta_5.
\]

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{any distinct-ray ground `3+3` equilibrium in an open semicircle has word }
+--++-
\text{ or its global complement.}
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

Reversing the ray order changes this word into its global complement, so (2.3) is
orientation-independent.

---

## 3. Exact enumeration of all uniform `H6` order types

For a noncollinear `H6` configuration, only the radial order data are needed from the
reciprocal coordinates.

Fix the convex-hull chirotope on vertices `0,...,5` with positive orientation on every
increasing hull triple. For the interior point `6`, the sorted signs

\[
\chi(i,j,6),\qquad 0\le i<j\le5,
\]

determine its cell in the arrangement of hull lines. Interior location fixes the six
boundary signs

\[
\chi(i,i+1,6)=+1\quad(0\le i<5),
\qquad
\chi(0,5,6)=-1.
\]

Nine signs remain free. The verifier checks all `2^9=512` assignments against the
rank-three Grassmann-Pluecker chirotope axiom, exhaustively over all ordered five-element
subsets. Exactly

\[
\boxed{26}
\]

uniform single-element extensions survive, with 26 distinct interior-ray insertion
patterns around the six hull vertices.

For each of those 26 exact order types, the verifier then checks:

- all 15 perfect matchings `M` on `{1,...,6}`;
- both global complements of the ground word (2.3); and
- all `2^5` choices of which global sign color is the local degree-two class at hull
  vertices `1,...,5`.

At a non-ground hull vertex the matching mate is deleted from the six raw rays; the
local degree-two sign class is then required to occupy positions two and four of the
remaining five rays. Edge signs are undirected, so the assignments from the two
endpoints of every reciprocal edge must agree.

The exact count is

\[
\boxed{0\text{ compatible signings}.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

No metric coordinate, numerical angle, or floating-point tolerance is used.

Therefore a uniform reciprocal `H6` support-six block is impossible.

---

## 4. The only possible `H6` collinearity

It remains to remove the nonuniform case.

The previous support-six theorem proves that no three non-ground reciprocal points are
collinear. The six hull vertices are strictly convex, so three hull vertices cannot be
collinear either. Hence any collinear triple in `H6` must contain the unique interior
point `6` and the ground point `0`:

\[
0,6,j.
\]

If the reciprocal edge `6j` were present, then at physical pentagon `F_6` the anchor
edge `60` and relation edge `6j` would be projectively parallel. The bounded-pentagon
no-parallel-side theorem forbids that. Thus

\[
\boxed{6j\in M.}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Since `6` is interior and `0,j` are hull points, `6` lies on the hull diagonal segment
`0j`. In particular `j` cannot be the boundary neighbors `1` or `5`, so

\[
j\in\{2,3,4\}.
\tag{4.2}
\]

At the ground point the raw unit directions for `j` and `6` coincide.

They cannot have opposite reciprocal signs. If they did, those two ground-force terms
would cancel. The remaining four distinct unit directions lie in the same open
semicircle and would split `2+2`, forcing

\[
u_a+u_b=u_c+u_d
\]

for two disjoint pairs. But for two non-antipodal unit vectors, the sum uniquely
determines the unordered pair: its direction is the pair's angular bisector and its
norm is `2 cos(alpha/2)`, where `alpha<pi` is their angular separation. Hence the two
pairs would be identical, contradicting distinctness.

Therefore the coincident ground rays have the same sign. After a global sign reversal,
both are positive. Since the ground split remains `3+3`, exactly one of the other four
ground rays is positive.

---

## 5. Exact enumeration of the collinear walls

A configuration with exactly the collinearity `0,6,j` can be perturbed slightly to
either side of the line `0j` without changing any other orientation sign. Therefore it
lies on a codimension-one wall between two uniform extensions from Section 3 which
differ only in `chi(0,j,6)`.

The exact chirotope enumeration finds

\[
\boxed{14}
\]

such walls:

\[
4\text{ for }j=2,
\qquad
6\text{ for }j=3,
\qquad
4\text{ for }j=4.
\tag{5.1}
\]

For every wall the matching is forced to contain `j6`; the other four non-ground
vertices have three possible pairings. The verifier checks all of them, all four choices
of the third ground-positive ray, both global complements, and all `2^5` non-ground
local `2+3` phases.

At hull vertex `j` the ray tied with the ground direction is precisely the missing edge
`j6`, so deleting the matching mate removes the tie before the local five-ray
alternation rule is applied.

Again the exact count is

\[
\boxed{0\text{ compatible signings}.}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Thus the collinear `H6` branch is impossible as well.

---

## 6. Consequence

The preceding convex-`H7` theorem reduced a noncollapsed degree-pure support-six block
to reciprocal hull sizes five or six. Sections 2--5 eliminate six. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\text{every noncollapsed degree-pure support-six block has reciprocal hull size }5.
}
\tag{6.1}
\]

The support-six endpoint now has exactly two live mechanisms:

1. the noncollapsed reciprocal `H5` family, with the ground on the hull and two
   non-ground reciprocal points interior; and
2. the collapsed branch `y_1=\cdots=y_6\ne0`, equivalently six projectively parallel
   outside anchors with a `3+3` oriented-anchor split.

Both must be removed before the support minimum can rise from six to seven, so this note
does not by itself strengthen the global coefficient `n_1/6`.

The `H5` branch is now a finite rank-three order-type problem with two interior points.
The collapsed branch is an exact physical octahedral-train problem. Those are the next
support-six targets.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** proved the ground `3+3` semicircle sign-order lemma, exhaustively
classified all uniform convex-hexagon-plus-interior order types by exact chirotope
axioms, found no compatible reciprocal signing, then separately enumerated and excluded
the only allowed collinear walls. The noncollapsed degree-pure support-six branch is
therefore forced from `H5/H6` down to `H5` only. C-II remains open.
